# A HINGE THAT TURNS (M24).
#
# The M24 gate, and read the second half of it carefully:
#
#     a hinge turns, AND IT DOES NOT FALL APART WHILE TURNING.
#
# Turning needs one number. Not falling apart needs the solver to actually put
# the arm where the mate says it goes -- every angle, not just zero.
#
# Run it:
#   ep3d --script examples/hinge.ep3ds

# --- part 1: the bracket ------------------------------------------------------
#
# A plate with a pin standing up at one end.

sketch Plate
tool rect
click -50 -20
click 10 20
pad Bracket 8

sketch Pin
tool circle
click 0 0
click 6 0
pad Bracket 40

# ONE SOLID, not two. Two pads into one body that nothing consumes are two
# disjoint solids (ADR-M21-002), and an instance brings in the CHAIN TIP -- so
# without this the assembly below would get the pin on its own.
union Bracket

# THE MATE CONNECTOR, on the PART. This is where a connector belongs: define it
# once here and every instance of this bracket in every assembly has it
# (roadmap §21). It is not an assembly concept that happens to sit on a part.
#
# The three numbers after the position are where the connector's +Z POINTS, and
# that is the whole act of saying where the hinge is: a revolute turns about
# +Z. The pin stands up in +Z, so +Z is what this points along -- and saying it
# explicitly is the difference between a hinge on the pin and a hinge on
# whatever axis the default happened to be.
connector Pivot 0 0 40 0 0 1

echo --- the bracket ---
solve
measure
save build/hinge-bracket.ep3d

# --- part 2: the arm ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# A second body in the same document, so the file holds both and each insert
# below names which one it wants.

sketch Blade
tool rect
click -8 -8
click 90 8
pad Arm 10

# The arm's own connector, at the end that goes on the pin. Same axis: two
# connectors meeting in a revolute share their +Z, which is why both parts
# point theirs along the pin rather than along anything else.
connector Eye 0 0 0 0 0 1

echo --- the arm ---
solve
measure
save build/hinge-parts.ep3d

# --- the assembly -------------------------------------------------------------

assembly Hinge

insert Base build/hinge-bracket.ep3d Bracket
insert Swing build/hinge-parts.ep3d Arm

# SOMEWHERE HAS TO BE FIXED. A mate says where something is RELATIVE to
# something else, so a chain of them has to start from something that is not
# relative to anything. An assembly whose mates reach no ground is REFUSED --
# grounding the first instance in the list instead would make the answer depend
# on the order things were typed.
ground Base

# THE HINGE. Base's Pivot and Swing's Eye become the same place, free to turn
# about their shared +Z. The angle is given in DEGREES, like every other angle
# a script types, and stored in radians -- the same conversion `draft` makes.
mate revolute Elbow Base/Pivot Swing/Eye 0

echo --- closed, at 0 degrees ---
solve
measure

# --- and now turn it ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# One number changes. Everything downstream of it is worked out again, and the
# arm goes where the mate says -- which is the half of the gate that a picture
# cannot check and a centroid can.
#
# The arm is 98 x 16 x 10 with its Eye at the near end, so its centre of mass
# sits 41 mm out along the arm. At 0 degrees that is +41 in x; at 90 degrees it
# is +41 in y; at 180 it is -41 in x. The z never moves, because the hinge axis
# is z.

drive Elbow 90
echo --- turned a quarter ---
solve
measure

drive Elbow 180
echo --- turned a half ---
solve
measure

# The connectors stay together at every angle. If they did not, the arm would
# fly off in a way that the volume would never notice and the centre of mass
# would report as a wrong number rather than as an error -- which is why the
# measurement above is the test and the picture is not.

save build/hinge.ep3da
